(Fixture for tests/290: a generated program that punches a NUL hole
 through its own captured stdout and THEN fails a ledger assertion -
 fuzzer defect 14, reproduced from vox-notes/env-a-seed-40252.

 The mechanism is the one the campaign hit by chance. The harness
 captures a run by pointing the child's fd 1 at a file, so a program
 that opens "/dev/stdout" for writing reopens that same file at offset
 zero and truncates it, while the descriptor its ordinary Prints go
 through keeps its own offset. Everything printed before the reopen is
 discarded, everything printed after it lands past the short line the
 reopen wrote, and the gap between them reads back as NULs.

 The ASSERT line is therefore on the far side of a hole that holds no
 newline, which is exactly the capture the scan used to give up on.)
Print "the postbag size 11".
Print "ASSERT BUF-12: expected 1 got 1 (this one held and kept going)".
Print "the postbag capacity 64".
open a file for writing called 'the second view of stdout' at "/dev/stdout".
Write "fmt4 hello4 0\n" to 'the second view of stdout'.
Close 'the second view of stdout'.
If 1 is equal to 1 then, Print "ASSERT ENV-06: expected 9 got -1", Exit 95. Print "unreachable".
Print "unreachable".
